  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several device layouts for a flexible optoelectronic element that is bent in different directions while light enters through a transparent top electrode and current is collected at an opaque bottom electrode. Across the schematics, red arrows indicate the main direction of transport or electric field, while red X marks highlight regions that appear electrically blocked, inactive, or poorly contacted during bending.\n\nThe main message is that bending axis and electrode placement strongly affect which parts of the active layer remain functional. In some curved or folded geometries, only a narrow band near the center contributes effectively, whereas the outer sidewalls or edge regions become suppressed. Other designs reorient the contacts and clamps so that charge or current flows laterally instead of vertically, changing the active region and reducing loss under deformation.\n\nThe bottom row broadens this idea to arrays or segmented modules, showing multiple small units arranged in series or around a curved surface. These configurations seem intended to maintain operation over large curvature by distributing strain and preserving conductive pathways. Overall, the figure compares how mechanical bending reshapes the usable area and transport pathways in transparent-electrode flexible devices.",
